const MIDEAST_1973 = `The Middle East since the Six-Day War (June 1967): Israel holds the Sinai to the Suez Canal, the Gaza Strip, the West Bank with East Jerusalem and the Golan Heights. The Suez Canal has been closed since 1967; the War of Attrition along it ended with a ceasefire in August 1970. Israel's line on the canal is the Bar-Lev Line, a chain of strongpoints behind a sand rampart; on the Golan, an anti-tank ditch and two brigades. Egypt (Anwar Sadat, president since Nasser's death in 1970) expelled most of its Soviet advisers in July 1972 but keeps Soviet arms: SA-2, SA-3 and mobile SA-6 missiles, Sagger anti-tank missiles and bridging equipment. Syria (Hafez al-Assad) is armed by Moscow too. Saudi Arabia (King Faisal) has warned Washington that oil may be used as a weapon. Israel (Golda Meir; Moshe Dayan at defence; David Elazar chief of staff) trusts in "the concept": Egypt will not attack until it has the aircraft to strike Israel's airfields. Jordan (King Hussein) stayed out of war since Black September (1970). Lebanon shelters the PLO (Yasser Arafat) after its expulsion from Jordan. Iraq (al-Bakr, with Saddam Hussein as vice-president) nationalised its oil in 1972. Iran (the Shah) is America's policeman of the Gulf, its troops helping Sultan Qaboos against the Dhofar rebels in Oman. Libya's Gaddafi took the Aouzou Strip of Chad this year.`;
const POWERS_1973 = `The United States: President Richard Nixon, re-elected in a landslide in 1972, is sinking in the Watergate affair (the Senate hearings ran all summer; the special prosecutor wants the White House tapes); Vice-President Spiro Agnew is under investigation for bribery; Henry Kissinger became Secretary of State on 22 September and remains National Security Adviser. The last US troops left Vietnam in March under the Paris Peace Accords of 27 January; Congress cut off funds for bombing in Indochina from 15 August. The dollar floats since the end of Bretton Woods (1971–73). Détente: Nixon visited Beijing and Moscow in 1972; SALT I and the ABM Treaty were signed; Brezhnev visited the US in June 1973; the Conference on Security and Co-operation in Europe opened in Helsinki in July. The Soviet Union: Leonid Brezhnev, General Secretary, with Kosygin as premier, Podgorny as head of state, Gromyko abroad and Grechko at defence; Soviet Jews press to emigrate. China: Mao Zedong is old and ill; Zhou Enlai governs, Deng Xiaoping was restored as vice-premier in April; the Gang of Four leads the radicals; China and the Soviet Union have been enemies since the border war of 1969. Western Europe: the EEC took in Britain, Ireland and Denmark in January; Edward Heath's Britain faces the miners; Willy Brandt's Ostpolitik made treaties with Moscow, Warsaw and East Germany, and both Germanys joined the UN on 18 September; Georges Pompidou is gravely ill. Japan (Kakuei Tanaka) imports nearly all its oil.`;
const WORLD_1973 = `Indochina: despite the Paris Accords, North Vietnamese troops (about 150,000) stay in the South and the war goes on at a lower level; President Thieu holds the cities and most of the people; the Provisional Revolutionary Government holds pockets in the Central Highlands, along the Cambodian border and in the Mekong Delta. In Cambodia Lon Nol's Khmer Republic holds Phnom Penh and the provincial towns against the Khmer Rouge, who hold most of the countryside; American bombing ended on 15 August. In Laos a ceasefire since February; a coalition government with the Pathet Lao is being negotiated. Africa: Portugal (Marcelo Caetano) fights three colonial wars with 150,000 troops – in Angola (MPLA, FNLA, UNITA), Mozambique (FRELIMO, strong in Cabo Delgado, Niassa and Tete) and Guinea (the PAIGC, which holds much of the countryside and declared the Republic of Guinea-Bissau on 24 September; its founder Amílcar Cabral was murdered in January); discontented young officers have begun to meet as the Captains' Movement. Rhodesia (Ian Smith) faces ZANU's guerrillas in the north-east since December 1972; South Africa (Vorster) helps both. Ethiopia's old emperor faces famine in Wollo and the Eritrean fronts. Latin America: in Chile General Augusto Pinochet's junta overthrew Salvador Allende on 11 September and rules by terror; Juan Perón won Argentina's election on 23 September and takes office on 12 October; Uruguay's parliament was closed in June. Asia: India (Indira Gandhi) is supreme after the 1971 war that made Bangladesh; Pakistan (Bhutto) is rebuilding; the Shah and Saudi Arabia lead OPEC, which meets the oil companies in Vienna on 8 October to demand a higher price. Europe: Greece's junta under Papadopoulos is crumbling; Spain's Franco is 80, with Admiral Carrero Blanco as prime minister.`;

